From c963eab43b5bc81c2cc8101fed1cddd5fc82b8c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lakr <git@qaq.wiki>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 12:24:01 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 24/24] Answer a register read after the writes queued before
 it

The 14.6.1 warm boot of 2026-11-09 (~/codex-work/runs/reg2-14-warm)
logged the protocol handshake at 2.4 s and nothing after it for twelve
minutes: no root FIFO packet, no display, no IOMobileFramebuffer start
in the guest, WindowServer crashing for want of a display. No thread was
stuck: the vCPUs were halted in KVM, the main loop was in ppoll, and the
drain worker (the thread left named qemu-system-aar, since QEMU's
"reims-vgpu-drain" is one character past the kernel's limit) was in its
condition wait with nothing to do. The guest had simply stopped asking.

The guest driver writes the protocol version it speaks and reads the
register straight back; what it reads selects its feature set. A gfx
write that finds the device lock taken, here by a 4 ms poll for a few
microseconds, goes into the ingress queue for the drain to apply, and a
read did not look at that queue: one that found the lock free again
read the state as it was before the write. The warm boot's log shows the
write applied by the drain (protocol_version and the tranche's level
lines in one millisecond, where the cold boot's direct write was 8 ms
ahead of its first tranche), and no device_lock_busy line, so the read
took the lock and answered 0. The guest gave the device up there. The
queue predates this branch; the caches the warm boot kept are first
read 10 s later and played no part.

- The negotiated version is published, lock-free, by the vCPU that
  writes it, before the write can queue, and its read is served from
  there (GfxRegs::version_live, kept across reset and cleared by it).
- Any other read that can take the lock applies the queued writes
  first, in order, as the drain would have; the lock order stays ingress
  then inner. A read that cannot take the lock still answers from the
  cache, and its device_lock_busy line now says how many writes were
  queued.

Tests (lib, --test-threads=1): 2311 passed, 0 failed. New: a
version written while the lock is held reads back negotiated both while
it is held and once it is free, before any drain, and 0 after a reset; a
read applies two queued writes to one register in order and leaves the
queue empty. Without the change both read 0 (checked). No guest was
booted.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
